---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ConciergeCall from "../../components/ConciergeCall.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import ServiceSchema from "../../components/ServiceSchema.astro";

const facts = [
  { label: "Rate", value: "$600 / hour" },
  { label: "Format", value: "Zoom, confidential" },
  { label: "Parties", value: "Owner, board, or neighbor" },
  { label: "My role", value: "Neutral third party" },
];

import FaqBlock from "../../components/FaqBlock.astro";
import InlineScheduler from "../../components/InlineScheduler.astro";
import LeadCapture from "../../components/LeadCapture.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="HOA Dispute Mediation in Florida · Pro Se Fair Play Mediation"
  description="Private mediation for homeowners association disputes in Florida. $600 per hour combined rate, conducted via Zoom. A neutral mediator, not a law firm."
  ogImage="/og/hoa-mediation.jpg"
  heroImage="/three_people_table_argue.jpg"
>
  <ServiceSchema slug="hoa-mediation" />

  <!-- ========================== HEADER ========================== -->
  <header class="svc-header">
    <div class="container svc-header-inner">
      <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ href: "/services", label: "Services" }]} current="HOA Disputes" tone="dark" />
      <h1 class="display svc-title reveal reveal-d2">
        Homeowners Association <span class="display-italic">Disputes</span><span class="display-italic">.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede reveal reveal-d3">
        Private mediation for owners, boards and neighbors who would rather
        settle a community dispute across a table than escalate it. Conducted
        via Zoom, structured, confidential.
      </p>
      <div class="svc-header-ctas reveal reveal-d4">
        <a class="btn btn-primary svc-header-cta" href="/book">
          Book a Free, Private Consultation
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ========================== AT-A-GLANCE ========================== -->
  <section class="svc-facts">
    <div class="container">
      <dl class="facts-grid">
        {facts.map((f) => (
          <div class="fact">
            <dt class="fact-label smallcaps">{f.label}</dt>
            <dd class="fact-value display">{f.value}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="facts-note">
        The $600 hourly rate is a <strong>combined rate</strong> for the
        session rather than a charge per side, and it is commonly divided
        evenly between the parties. One side may also pay in full. Session
        structure, scheduling and the refund policy follow my{" "}
        <a class="link-underline" href="/services/hourly-mediation">Hourly Mediation</a>{" "}
        service, and every price is listed on{" "}
        <a class="link-underline" href="/pricing">pricing</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ========================== PROSE ========================== -->
  <section class="svc-section svc-section-alt">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <div class="svc-prose">
        <p>
          Community disputes are rarely about the thing they appear to be
          about. A disagreement over a fence line, a paint color or a late
          assessment has usually been running for months by the time anyone
          calls a mediator, and by then the parties are neighbors who have
          stopped speaking to each other and still have to live on the same
          street. Mediation is a structured way to put that conversation back
          on a footing where something can actually be settled.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is <strong>private mediation, not court-ordered work</strong>.
          An owner and a board, two owners, or a board and a vendor sit down
          with one neutral mediator outside the court system. In many cases
          Florida law requires that attempt before a dispute can be filed at
          all, which is set out below.
        </p>

        <h2>Florida Law and HOA Disputes: Why Mediation Comes First</h2>
        <p>
          Florida Statute 720.311 establishes a statutory default requiring
          mediation before litigation for most disputes between a homeowners
          association and its members, including conflicts over use and
          enjoyment of a parcel, architectural approvals, rule enforcement, and
          access to meetings or records. This means that in the majority of
          cases, parties are required to attempt mediation before either side
          can file a lawsuit.
        </p>
        <p>
          However, every association is different. The specific bylaws and
          governing documents of your community may include additional
          procedures, modified timelines, or alternative dispute resolution
          language that supersedes the general statutory default. Because of
          this, it is essential to review your association's governing
          documents alongside the statutory requirements before determining the
          correct path forward.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Pro Se Fair Play Mediation, we help homeowners and associations
          navigate this process with clarity. Whether your dispute involves a
          proposed improvement, a disagreement among members, or a conflict
          with the board, we can help you determine whether mediation is
          required, guide you through the process, and help you avoid the cost
          and delay of unnecessary litigation.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are facing a homeowners association dispute, the right first
          step is rarely an attorney. It is understanding what the statute
          requires and what your community's governing documents say.
        </p>
        {/* Added, not part of the supplied copy: the statute's own exclusion.
            It sits here because the subject-matter list immediately below
            offers assessments and fines as things commonly mediated, and
            without this a reader could take the presuit requirement to cover
            a fine collection dispute. Remove on the owner's word. */}
        <p>
          One limit worth knowing: the presuit requirement does not extend to
          the collection of assessments, fines and other financial obligations,
          or to actions enforcing a prior mediation settlement. Those can still
          be mediated privately when both sides want to.
        </p>

        <h2>What owners and boards bring to the table</h2>
        <p>
          The subject matter varies, and every community's governing documents
          are its own. Matters commonly mediated include:
        </p>
        <ul class="svc-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Covenant, rule and restriction disagreements.</strong>
            Where the parties read the same provision two different ways and
            want a workable resolution rather than a ruling.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Architectural and alteration requests.</strong> Additions,
            fences, sheds, paint, landscaping, solar, screens and other
            improvements, whether approval, denial or conditions are in
            dispute.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Assessments, fines and payment arrangements.</strong>
            Including a schedule for bringing an account current.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Maintenance and repair responsibility.</strong> Who
            maintains what, common elements and shared structures, water
            intrusion, drainage and damage between neighboring properties.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Use and occupancy friction.</strong> Parking, pets,
            leasing and short-term rentals, noise, nuisance, trash, signage and
            guest use of shared amenities.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Records requests and communication breakdown.</strong>
            Along with board and committee friction that has made ordinary
            business difficult to conduct.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Who sits at the table</h2>
        <p>
          Whoever has the authority to settle. On the association side that
          usually means a board member or a manager with authority to agree to
          terms, and on the owner's side the owners themselves. Bringing
          someone who has to take the outcome back to a third party for
          approval is the most common reason a productive session ends without
          an agreement, so it is worth sorting out before the date is set.
          Either side may have its own attorney involved, and associations
          frequently do.
        </p>

        <h2>What mediation is not</h2>
        <p>
          I am a neutral. I am not an attorney, I do not represent either side,
          and this is not a law firm. That means I will not interpret your
          governing documents for you, tell you whether a rule or a fine is
          enforceable, say what a court would likely do with the dispute, or
          tell you whether the terms on the table are worth accepting. Those
          are legal questions, and they belong with an attorney who represents
          you.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I do is run the process: keep the discussion organized, make
          sure both sides are genuinely heard, and hold the session to the
          matters the parties came to resolve.
        </p>

        <h2>If you reach agreement</h2>
        <p>
          The terms are written up while everyone is still at the table, so
          that what was agreed is captured accurately rather than reconstructed
          a week later. Each side is free to have the written terms reviewed by
          its own attorney before signing, and I encourage it. I am also a
          Notary Public, so anything arising from the session that needs
          notarizing can be handled at the time.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the parties settle only part of it, that is a normal outcome and
          not a wasted session. Narrowing a dispute to the one issue still in
          contention changes what everything after it costs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ========================== CTA ========================== -->
  <section class="cta">
    <div class="container-narrow cta-inner">
      <ConciergeCall />
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary cta-btn" href="/book">
          Book a Free, Private Consultation
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="scheduling-note">Flexible Scheduling Available: Evening and Weekend Mediations to Accommodate Your Busy Life.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Calendar inline, not a link to one — this is where the reader is
      already at peak intent. Lazy-loaded on scroll. */}
  <FaqBlock set="hoa-mediation" />
  <InlineScheduler source="svc-hoa-mediation" />
  <LeadCapture source="svc-hoa-mediation" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ---- Identical pattern to divorce-mediation.astro for visual consistency ---- */
  .svc-header {
    position: relative;
    padding-block: clamp(5rem, 7vw + 2rem, 8rem) clamp(3rem, 4vw + 1rem, 5rem);
    color: var(--color-cream);
    background-color: var(--color-navy-deep);
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(135deg,
        rgba(15, 27, 50, 0.9) 0%,
        rgba(27, 42, 74, 0.78) 50%,
        rgba(27, 42, 74, 0.9) 100%),
      url('/three_people_table_argue.jpg');
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .svc-header::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: auto 0 0 0;
    height: 4rem;
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, var(--color-cream));
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .svc-header-inner { position: relative; max-width: 64rem; z-index: 1; }
  .svc-title { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: var(--step-5); line-height: 1.02; color: var(--color-cream); letter-spacing: -0.025em; max-width: 22ch; font-weight: 400; text-shadow: 0 1px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9); }
  .svc-title .display-italic { color: var(--color-gold); font-weight: 400; }
  .svc-header .lede { margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 60ch; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-cream) 85%, transparent); text-shadow: 0 1px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9); }
  .svc-header-cta { margin-top: 0; }
  .svc-header-ctas {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .svc-facts { padding-block: var(--space-xl); background: var(--color-cream); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-divider); }
  .facts-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 1.5rem 2rem; margin: 0; }
  .fact { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.35rem; }
  .fact-label { font-size: var(--step-0); color: var(--color-gold-deep); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.24em; }
  .fact-value { margin: 0; font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-navy); font-variation-settings: "opsz" 24; line-height: 1.2; }
  .facts-note { margin-top: 1.5rem; padding-top: 1.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-divider); font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: var(--step-sm); line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-ink-soft); max-width: 70ch; font-style: italic; }
  .facts-note strong { color: var(--color-navy); font-weight: 600; font-style: normal; }
  .facts-note a { font-style: normal; }

  .svc-section { padding-block: var(--space-2xl); }
  .svc-section-alt { background: var(--color-cream-warm); }
  .svc-prose { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-soft); max-width: 60ch; }
  .svc-prose p + p { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .svc-prose strong { color: var(--color-navy); font-weight: 500; }

  /* Subheads and the topic list, matching divorce-mediation: this page is an
     explanatory hub rather than one block of prose, so it needs the same
     structure that page needed. */
  .svc-prose h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--color-navy);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .svc-list {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .svc-list li { padding-left: 0.25rem; }
  .svc-list li::marker { color: var(--color-gold-deep); }

  .cta { position: relative; padding-block: var(--space-lg); background: var(--color-cream); color: var(--color-ink); overflow: hidden; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-divider); }
  .cta::before { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0 0 auto 0; height: 1px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, var(--color-gold) 40%, var(--color-gold) 60%, transparent); opacity: 0.7; }
  .cta-actions { margin-top: 0; display: flex; justify-content: center; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .cta-btn { background: var(--color-navy); color: var(--color-cream); border-color: var(--color-navy); }
  .cta-btn:hover { background: var(--color-navy-deep); color: var(--color-cream); border-color: var(--color-gold); }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .facts-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .facts-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
